{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Drift Ice Return: Pseudomonas Wake-Up in the North Water Polynya",
    "date": "2026-11-22",
    "lesson": "The North Water Polynya (Pikialasorsuaq) between Greenland and Ellesmere Island is a patch of open water ringed by ice that stays biologically active through the Arctic transition seasons because upwelling keeps nutrients coming even while the sun is barely above the horizon. Psychrotolerant bacteria here, Pseudomonas and Colwellia-type generalists, spend weeks near their cystBelow threshold, idling at low respiration, then surge the moment a diatom pulse or polynya upwelling delivers lipid-rich material. The lesson: cold does not stop growth, it just throttles the Q10-scaled metabolic rate and stretches out every response, so a polar generalist's strategy is patience, cyst dormancy, and opportunistic feeding rather than speed.",
    "citation": "Tremblay et al. 2002, North Water Polynya nutrient dynamics; Deming 2002 psychrophile physiology",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "North Water Polynya (Pikialasorsuaq), Nares Strait/Baffin Bay, Arctic spring transition"
  },
